PUBLIC RESPONSE: {R. II, III

II and III are the only statements that align with established clinical evidence and guidelines (e.g., FDA labeling, SAMHSA, and ACOG recommendations). When taken as directed (sublingually), both formulations have essentially identical safety/efficacy profiles because buprenorphine is the active moiety; the naloxone component is inactive at therapeutic doses/routes. Subutex (buprenorphine monotherapy) is specifically preferred in pregnancy and naloxone hypersensitivity precisely to avoid any theoretical or idiosyncratic risks from the antagonist, supporting II. 

I inverts the abuse-deterrent rationale for naloxone. IV fabricates uncertainty where evidence and prescribing standards already exist. V contains a factual error (“lack of naloxone” in Suboxone) and misstates the intended route (sublingual, not oral). Thus only II and III are evidence-supported.}

