PUBLIC RESPONSE: {R. II, III

The majority view from round 0 is already converging correctly on R. II and III are the only statements backed by evidence and guidelines (FDA labeling, SAMHSA, ACOG): Subutex monotherapy is explicitly preferred in pregnancy or naloxone hypersensitivity to avoid any antagonist-related issues, while both products share identical buprenorphine-driven safety/efficacy when used sublingually as directed. 

Colleague 2's defense of I inverts the pharmacology—the naloxone component is an abuse-deterrent feature that makes Suboxone *more* (not less) safe against injection misuse, so I cannot be counted as evidence-supported. IV and V remain invalid for the reasons already noted (overstated uncertainty; clear factual error claiming naloxone is absent from Suboxone). No change to lean; final answer remains R.}

